  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"113\" data-end=\"225\"><strong data-start=\"113\" data-end=\"153\">Magnesium measurement in body fluids</strong> (such as pleural, peritoneal/ascitic, or cerebrospinal fluid) helps in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"227\" data-end=\"532\">\n<li data-start=\"227\" data-end=\"292\">\n<p data-start=\"229\" data-end=\"292\"><strong data-start=\"229\" data-end=\"268\">Identifying the nature of effusions</strong> (transudate vs exudate)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"293\" data-end=\"361\">\n<p data-start=\"295\" data-end=\"361\">Detecting <strong data-start=\"305\" data-end=\"327\">local inflammation</strong>, <strong data-start=\"329\" data-end=\"342\">infection</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"361\">malignancy</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"362\" data-end=\"438\">\n<p data-start=\"364\" data-end=\"438\">Monitoring <strong data-start=\"375\" data-end=\"411\">magnesium loss or redistribution</strong> in critically ill patients</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"439\" data-end=\"532\">\n<p data-start=\"441\" data-end=\"532\">Evaluating suspected <strong data-start=\"462\" data-end=\"502\">urinary or gastrointestinal leakages</strong> (especially in ascitic fluid)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"534\" data-end=\"617\">It is typically interpreted alongside serum magnesium and other fluid electrolytes.</p>\n<p data-start=\"534\" data-end=\"617\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Clinical Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Interpretation of Elevated Fluid Magnesium</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Infection or inflammation</strong></td>\n<td>Cell lysis and increased permeability may cause elevated magnesium in exudative fluids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Malignant effusions</strong></td>\n<td>Tumor breakdown can release intracellular magnesium into surrounding fluid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Urine leak into body cavity</strong></td>\n<td>In cases like urinary ascites or bladder rupture, urine (rich in magnesium) can raise fluid magnesium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Magnesium-containing dialysis fluids</strong></td>\n<td>In peritoneal dialysis patients, contamination with dialysate may raise levels</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"534\" data-end=\"617\">&nbsp;</p>",
